Optimized and sensitive biomarker detection has recently been shown to have a critical impact on quality of diagnosis and medical care options. In this research study, polyoxometalate-γ-cyclodextrin metal−organic framework (POM– $-\gamma\mathrm{CD}$ MOF) was utilized as an electrocatalyst to fabricate highly selective sensors to detect insitu released dopamine. The POM– $-\gamma\mathrm{CD}$ MOF produced multiple modes of signals for dopamine including elec­ trochemical, colorimetric, and smartphone read-outs. Real-time quantitative monitoring of SH-SY5Y neuroblas­ toma cellular dopamine production was successfully demonstrated under various stimuli at different time intervals. The POM-CD MOF sensor and linear regression model were used to develop a smartphone read-out platform, which converts dopamine visual signals to digital signals within a few seconds. Ultimately, POM– $-\gamma\mathrm{CD}$ MOFs can play a significant role in the diagnosis and treatment of various diseases that involve dopamine as a significant biomarker.
## 1. Introduction
Real-time quantitative biomarker detection in living biological sys­ tems has recently attracted significant attention because it can give users dynamic information about the intricately linked biological processes connected to a given biomarker (Bukhamsin et al., 2022; Lyu et al., 2019). These sensing technologies are essential for comprehending the physiology, pathology, and pharmacology of the biomarker and are anticipated to be fundamental in the development of the next generation of clinical and non-clinical diagnostics, continuous monitoring, drug discovery, and drug screening (Mota et al., 2021; Parolo et al., 2020). Such real-time technologies are vital for neurotransmitters. A well-known biomarker for many neurodegenerative conditions (Gar­ cia-Pardo et al., 2021) as well as cancers of the adrenal gland, including neuroblastoma (Senel et al., 2020b) and pheochromocytoma (Wang et al., 2015), is dopamine (DA).
Among a plethora of dopamine-sensing techniques, electrochemical methods proved to be the best choice due to their quick response time, high sensitivity, low cost, and—most importantly—the simplicity of dopamine detection by electro-oxidation (Berni et al., 2022; Mani et al., 2016; Moon et al., 2018). However, there are some drawbacks to DA detection, such as the possibility of false negatives if the concentration of other species in the real samples such as uric acid and ascorbic acid is high (Cho et al., 2020). Consequently, few sensors can perform contin­ uous monitoring in real-world settings. Many of DA sensors currently on the market have low sensitivity, poor selectivity, matrix effects, and low signal-to-noise ratios (Campuzano et al., 2020). Functional materials that can selectively interact with analytes and communicate biological information in multiple signal mediums are potential candidates for mitigating the aforementioned issues and improving data precision and accuracy (Ling et al., 2018). Metal oxides, peptides, polymers, and carbon-based nanomaterials such as nanotubes, and graphene oxide have all been used to electrochemically detect dopamine (Farajikhah et al., 2019; Liu and Liu, 2021). However, such systems could not effectively address the issue of interference and dopamine detection sensitivity to the best of our knowledge.
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are a promising class of materials that have excelled in numerous applications—including gas storage, separation, catalysis, batteries, and sensors—because of their high porosity, large surface area, and relative ease of custom-made structures (Nicks et al., 2021). On the other hand, polyoxometalates (POMs), a different class of materials, have attracted attention due to their appli­ cability in a variety of fields, including catalysis, storage, medicine, and sensors because of their significant redox behavior and high thermal stability (Gumerova and Rompel, 2018). However, their low specific surface area, poor homogeneous catalyst recyclability, and low POM active site exposure of POM-based catalysts have resulted in poor per­ formance in all applications (Ma et al., 2018). Using POM as a node when creating an MOF may result in a material that combines the excellent properties of both materials, as has been reported in numerous recent studies (Liu et al., 2021; Zhang et al., 2021). Each distinct ma­ terial was used for dopamine sensing and demonstrated a partial reso­ lution for the interference problem (Jiao et al., 2018).
In addition to traditional metal carboxylate clusters, scientists have recently focused a great deal of attention on the assembly of a POM with an organic macrocycle, particularly cyclodextrins (CDs), to create POMorganic frameworks (POM-CD MOFs) (Yang et al., 2020a; Yang et al., 2019). When such a hybrid system is formulated, it frequently has better properties than its individual molecular building blocks (MBBs). For instance, a three-dimensional framework was produced by the self-assembly of two MBBs, namely $\alpha$ -CD with intrinsic porosity and PW12, through the counter-cation effect. These combined materials have been tested as lithium-ion battery anode materials, and they exhibit exceptional Li storage behavior (Yang et al., 2019). Such intrinsically porous hybrid materials can also improve the interactions between organic substrates and reactant molecules, which can make it easier for molecules to access the catalytic sites in heterogeneous catalysis due to the rapid fertility of substrate molecules and the repel­ lence of product molecules (Zhu et al., 2016). Shu et al. developed a nickel metal–organic framework modified Au nanoparticle-carbon nanotubes based composite (Ni-MOF/AuNPs/CNTs) for DA detections released from C6 living cells (Shu et al., 2020). Porphyrin-based MOF-525 nanocrystals coated with PEDOT nanotubes were found to be useful for real-time determination of DA released from living pheo­ chromocytoma cells (Huang et al., 2017). However, POM-CD based MOFs have never been reported for DA analysis in live cells to the best of our knowledge.
In this study, we describe the use of POM-γCD MOF material as an electrocatalyst for dopamine sensing. Laboratory samples, serum sam­ ples, and SH-SY5Y neuroblastoma cells were used to test the sensing capabilities, thereby opening a new avenue for understanding neuro­ degenerative diseases. This platform offers the chance to create a multimodal sensor that not only offers visual signals but also quantitative signals. Our development of a specific smartphone app using a straightforward linear regression algorithm that can read color changes and translate them into quantitative digital signals was also influenced by the novel property of the POM- $\gamma\mathrm{CD}$ MOF material.
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1. Synthesis of POM- ${\gamma C D}$ MOF
The POM- $\cdot\gamma\mathrm{CD}$ MOF material was prepared by following our previ­ ously reported procedure with a slight modification (Fig. 1A) (Yang et al., 2020a). About $50~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of $85\%$ $\mathrm{H_{3}P O_{4}}$ and $\mathrm{MoNa_{2}O_{4}{\cdot}2H_{2}O}$ $242\mathrm{mg}$ , $1.0~\mathrm{\mmol})$ was added successively upon stirring to a solution of $\boldsymbol{\upgamma}$ -cyclodextrin $\mathrm{130~mg}$ , 0.1 mmol) in $5~\mathrm{mL}$ of $1.0\mathrm{~M~KCH_{3}C O O/C H_{3}}.$ COOH buffer $\mathrm{(pH5.0)}$ . The solution was then stirred for 1 h at $80^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The turbid white solution was cooled to room temperature and filtered, and the filtrate was kept for crystallization under ambient conditions. The needle-shaped, colorless crystalline product POM- $\ensuremath{\boldsymbol{\cdot}}\ensuremath{\boldsymbol{\gamma}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{CD}}$ MOF started to appear after two weeks and was collected by filtration and then air-dried. Yield: $0.281\mathrm{g}$ ( $70\%$ based on $\gamma$ -CD). The full chemical formula of POM- $\boldsymbol{\cdot}\gamma\mathrm{CD}$ MOF is $\mathrm{H_{4}K_{20}N a_{2}[P_{2}M o_{5}O_{23}]_{4}[\gamma-C D]_{2}\cdot2(C H_{3}C O O)\cdot48H_{2}O}.$
## 2.2. Fabrication of POM-γCD MOF modified laser-scribed graphene electrode
First, $0.5\%$ dispersion of Nafion was prepared from $5\%$ Nafion using ethanol as a dispersant. Then, $2.0~\mathrm{mg/mL}$ of POM- $\gamma\mathrm{CD}$ MOF was pre­ pared in $0.5\%$ Nafion. Nafion provide a strong adhesive quality to the material that allows it to bind to the electrode surface, preventing the leaching of material, as well as lending anti-fouling and anti-interfering properties. Approximately ${\mathsf{5.0}}\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of POM- $\gamma\mathrm{CD}$ MOF was drop-casted on the laser-scribed graphene electrode (LSGE) and dried at ambient con­ ditions to get POM- $\boldsymbol{\cdot}\gamma\mathrm{CD}$ MOF/LSGE.
## 2.3. SH-SY5Y cell culture
The cells were cultured into a 24-well plate at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $5\%{\mathrm{CO}}_{2}$ with $1.0\times10^{7}$ cells/mL density. The medium consisted of $5\%$ horse serum, $1\%$ penicillin-streptomycin, and $10\%$ fetal bovine serum. The number of cells was approximately $1\ \times\ 10^{6}\ \mathrm{cells/cm^{2}}$ , as measured by flow cytometry. Next, the cells were distributed in phosphate buffer saline (PBS) and transferred to an electrochemical cell. The $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ stimulation was used to depolarize the live cells and to induce the release of DA by exocytosis. Different concentrations of KCl were co-incubated with the cells, and the in-situ production of DA was subsequently monitored.
## 3. Results and DISCUSSIONS
## 3.1. Material characterizations
The colorless needle-shaped crystals of the POM- $\boldsymbol{\cdot}\gamma\mathrm{CD}$ MOF were obtained by the interaction of $\gamma{\mathrm{-CD}}$ with sodium molybdate dihydrate dissolved in potassium acetate buffer in the presence of $85\%$ phosphoric acid (Fig. S1A). It should be noted that potassium counter-cation $(\mathrm{K^{+}})$ played a vital role in connecting both MBBs (i.e., $\boldsymbol{\upgamma}$ -cyclodextrin with polyoxomolybdate) to form a three-dimensional porous hybrid frame­ work and balance the charge of the structure. The formula of the assynthesized POM- $\cdot\gamma$ -CD MOF structure $\mathrm{H_{4}K_{20}N a_{2}[P_{2}M o_{5}O_{23}]_{4}[\gamma}$ - $\mathrm{CD}]_{2^{\cdot}2}(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COO}){\cdot}48\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ was calculated on the basis of the singlecrystal structure (Fig. 1B & Fig. S2). Inductively coupled plasma opti­ cal emission spectroscopy (ICP-OES) data and elemental analysis of POM- $\ensuremath{\gamma}\ensuremath{\mathrm{CD}}$ MOF $(\%)$ : Calcd: K 9.71, Na 0.57, P 3.07, Mo 23.84, C 14.92; Found: K 9.41, Na 0.51, P 2.97, Mo 23.14, C 15.03.
The phase purity of POM- $\ensuremath{\gamma}\ensuremath{\mathrm{CD}}$ MOF was confirmed via powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) (Fig. 1C) (Yang et al., 2020a). Furthermore, the material was characterized by FT-IR (Fig. 1D). The strong absorption band of cyclodextrin $0{\mathrm{-H}}$ bonds emerges at 3454 and $3180~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . The peak at $2656\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ corresponds to the asymmetrical stretching vibration of $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{H}$ bonds, whereas the band at $1160~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ relates to the stretching vibration of glycosidic C–O–C and C–O bonds in polysaccharides (Soc­ rates, 2004). Peaks less than $904~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ can be attributed to terminal $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Mo}=0}$ bonds as well as bridging Mo–O–Mo bond stretching modes.
The thermal stability of the sample was tested using a thermogra­ vimetric analyzer (TGA) under nitrogen gas. The thermogram depicted three steps of weight loss. The weight loss of $14.7\%$ at $20{-}160~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ cor­ responds to the loss of non-coordinated and coordinated water mole­ cules (Fig. 1E). Additional weight loss covering the temperature range from 200 to $800^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ arises from decomposition of organic molecules and structural reorganization of the polyanion. The TGA curve gives a total weight loss of the cluster around $800^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ Interestingly, POM- $\ensuremath{\gamma}\ensuremath{\mathrm{CD}}$ MOF shows good solubility in most of the organic solvents, such as $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN}$ , MeOH, and $(\mathrm{CH}_{3})_{2}\mathrm{CO}$ , but it can decompose easily in water, as confirmed in the $^1\mathrm{HNMR}$ study (Fig. S3). The NMR spectra indicate that the characteristic peaks of $\gamma\mathrm{CD}$ match the cluster POM- $\boldsymbol{\cdot}\gamma\mathrm{CD}$ MOF peaks. All the characterization data are consistent with the previously reported POM-CD MOFs (Yang et al., 2020a; Yang et al., 2019).
The electrochemical behavior of the POM- $\ensuremath{\gamma}\ensuremath{\mathrm{CD}}$ MOF modified LSGE was tested: A pair of redox peaks were observed in the CVs that correspond to the redox reactions of POM components of the MOF (Fig. 1F). The POM materials are known for their oxidizing properties; the POM- $\gamma\mathrm{CD}$ MOF is expected to have an electrocatalytic property for oxidizing vital biomolecules (Boyd et al., 2017). The kinetics of the redox reactions of POM- $\boldsymbol{\cdot}\gamma\mathrm{CD}$ MOF showed surface confined redox pro­ cess (Fig. S4) (Yang et al., 2014). The ECSA of the POM- $\ensuremath{\gamma}\ensuremath{\mathrm{CD}}$ MOF/LSGE and unmodified LSGE were found to be 0.227 and $0.156~\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ , respec­ tively (Fig. S5). A $46\%$ increased ECSA was observed when POM- $\boldsymbol{\cdot}\gamma\mathrm{CD}$ MOF material was used. The enhanced ECSA likely originated from the contribution of the POM elements (Wang et al., 2020).
## 3.2. Electrocatalysis of POM- $\gamma C D$ MOF
Next, the electrocatalytic ability of the POM- $\gamma\mathrm{CD}$ MOF/LSGE to catalyze DA redox reaction was tested (Fig. 2A and Fig. S6). The CV of POM- $\cdot\gamma\mathrm{CD}$ MOF/LSGE displayed characteristic peaks corresponding to the redox reactions of the POM- $\boldsymbol{\cdot}\gamma\mathrm{CD}$ . However, a notable oxidation peak at $+\ 0.059\mathrm{~V~}$ and a reduction peak at $+\ 0.029\ \mathrm{V}$ were observed in presence of DA. The formal potential $(E^{\circ})$ was $+0.044\mathrm{V}$ , matching with the typical $2\mathrm{e}^{-}/2\mathrm{H}^{+}$ redox reactions of DA, responsible for the redox conversion of DA to dopamine- $o$ -quinone. Similar redox peaks were observed at LSGE, however with a $149\mathrm{mV}$ high overpotential and 1.9- fold lower currents. The CV results are consistent with DPV results (Fig. 2B). According to both CV and DPV results, the POM- $\boldsymbol{\cdot}\gamma\mathrm{CD}$ MOF is an excellent electrocatalyst for DA, facilitating the rate of the DA redox processes much faster. Interestingly, a quick color change from colorless to yellowish was observed when the DA solution was electrochemically treated with POM- $\cdot\gamma\mathrm{CD}$ MOF/LSGE (Inset, Fig. 2C). The change in color happened immediately after the solution was treated electrochemically with POM- $\boldsymbol{\cdot}\gamma\mathrm{CD}$ MOF/LSGE. Both CV and DPV electrochemical treat­ ments able to spur this color change, however, only when POM- $\boldsymbol{\cdot}\gamma\mathrm{CD}$ MOF was modified on the LSGE surface. This color change was not observed when the DA solution was treated with unmodified LSGE even after 10 cycles of CV. The color behavior was further analyzed by UV–visible spectroscopy.
The PBS containing $0\mathrm{mM}$ DA (curve b) or $300\upmu\mathrm{M}$ DA (curve d) were first electrochemically treated with POM- $\gamma\mathrm{CD}$ MOF/LSGE, then their optical images and UV–Visible spectra were analyzed (Fig. 2C). When DA was electrocatalytically oxidized by POM- $\boldsymbol{\gamma}\mathrm{CD}$ MOF/LSGE, the color of the electrochemical cell solution became yellowish immediately (inset d, Fig. 2C). The transformation of a colorless solution into a yellowish one shows that DA has been oxidized into its dopamine-oquinone. In the corresponding UV–visible spectra, a visible band at around $400\ \mathrm{nm}$ was observed for the DA solution that underwent elec­ trochemical treatment with POM-CD MOF/LSGE; this band matches the visible band of quinone (Fig. S7A). On the other hand, when DA was electrochemically treated with LSGE alone, neither a noticeable color change nor a visible band were seen (curve c, inset c, Fig. 2C). In addition, UV–visible spectra of the following solutions: DA, POM- $\cdot\gamma\mathrm{CD}$ MOF mixed with DA solution (without electrochemical treatment), and hydroquinone (HQ) were performed; none of them displayed the char­ acteristic visible band of quinone (Fig. S7).
Thus, the UV–visible results provide additional evidence for the rapid electrocatalytic conversion of DA to dopamine-o-quinone when POM-CD MOF was used. Based on the CV, DPV, and UV–visible results, we conclude that POM- $\boldsymbol{\gamma}\mathrm{CD}$ MOF is an excellent and sensitive electro­ catalyst to facilitate DA oxidation, thereby allowing for rapid DA detection via both visual and electrochemical signal readouts.
## 3.3. Quantitative and visual determination of dopamine
CVs and DPVs were employed for the determination of DA (Fig. 3A–D). In both CV (Fig. 3A) and DPV (Fig. 3B) data, a linear rise in current signal can be observed as the concentration of DA increased. Consistent signals are observed even for the lower concentration range of DA. DPV data was used to make a concentration plot which is given in
Fig. 3C, and its regression equation is, $\mathrm{{I}}\left(\upmu{\bf{A}}\right)=26.14$ [DA] $(\mathrm{nM})-16.35$ . The lowest concentration detected was $10\mathrm{nM}$ , and the linear range was found to be $50\mathrm{nM}$ to $1.0\mathrm{mM}$ ; these parameters are reliable for analyzing clinical samples. Moreover, the color change was prompt and occurred immediately upon voltammetry scans (Fig. 3E). Color change was observed starting from $50~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ up to $1.0~\mathrm{mM}$ . The time required for DA solution to change color on its own was typically between 3 and 4 days. However, the POM- $\ensuremath{\gamma}\ensuremath{\mathrm{CD}}$ MOF/LSGE in association with electrochemical perturbation facilitates the oxidation process to the point where the color change can occur in a matter of minutes. The analytical parameters of POM-γCD MOF/LSGE is comparable to the previous DA sensors (Hira et al., 2021; Huang et al., 2020; Li et al., 2021; Ognjanovi´c et al., 2021; Ramachandran et al., 2020; Santhosh Kumar et al., 2021; Shi et al., 2021; Shu et al., 2020) (Table S1). Notably, most of the previous reports use glassy carbon electrodes (GCE) or indium tin oxide (ITO), which are expensive, inflexible, and unsuitable for point-of-care (PoC) diagnostic applications. Here, we used LSGE, which is less expensive and dispos­ able, meeting the major requirement for performing PoC studies (Lahcen et al., 2020). A small sample volume of $75~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ is used. Most of the previous studies are based on one signal read-out. In this work, the signal read-outs are three including electrochemical, colorimetric and smartphone transducers. To the best of our knowledge, such multi-model DA sensors are scarcely reported in the literature.
## 3.4. High selectivity of POM-γCD MOF
Potential interferences with ascorbic acid (AA), uric acid (UA), Lcysteine (Cys), hydrogen peroxide $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2})$ , glucose (Glu), potassium chloride (KCl), norepinephrine (NP), and epinephrine (EP) were studied. Both electrochemical signals (Fig. 3D and Fig. S8) and visual signal (Fig. 3F–H and Fig. S9) were monitored and analyzed. In the visual analysis, AA, UA, Cys, and Glu presented no color change with POM- $\boldsymbol{\cdot}\gamma\mathrm{CD}$ MOF/LSGE when electrochemically treated. In fact, millimolar levels of AA displayed no color change, whereas even micro-molar levels of DA exhibited a color change observable to a naked eye. In other words, the presence of 1000-fold higher concentrations of AA, UA, Cys, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ Glu, and KCl compared to DA are not showing any visual signal interference, indicating high level of visual selectivity. The visual selectivity results are further supported by CV analysis (Fig. S8), the CV results are pre­ sented as a bar chart in Fig. 3D. The analytes, AA, UA, Cys, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and KCl are showing some level of matrix interferences when they are coexisting with DA and when the concentrations of these compounds are in the millimolar level; however, the interference level is in the acceptable range of $1{-}8\%$ . Overall, the visual and electrochemical analysis attesting that the POM- $\gamma\mathrm{CD}$ MOF has a special selectivity to DA against AA, UA, Cys, and Glu.
However, the MOF responded to EP and NP, suggesting that the POM- $\ensuremath{\gamma}\ensuremath{\mathrm{CD}}$ MOF material is an excellent electrocatalyst for oxidizing the catecholamines DA, EP, and NP. The results show that the presence of catechol groups in the analyte is necessary for the POM- $\cdot\gamma\mathrm{CD}$ MOF to facilitate the electrocatalytic-assisted colorimetric reaction. AA, UA, Glu, and Cys lacked the catechol groups necessary to initiate the catal­ ysis, making them unable to cause significant interferences. Determi­ nation of catecholamines in urine and blood is a vital test performed in clinical settings to screen for several diseases. In such tests, the POM$\boldsymbol{\upgamma}\mathrm{CD}$ MOF could offer excellent sensitivity and selectivity for catechol­ amines. The POM- $\ensuremath{\gamma}\ensuremath{\mathrm{CD}}$ MOF/LSGE has shown appreciable stability and reproducibility, which are discussed in supporting information (section S5, Fig. S10).
## 3.5. Validation in complex samples
The practical feasibility of the POM- $\boldsymbol{\cdot}\gamma\mathrm{CD}$ MOF/LSGE was tested in a synthetic human serum. The serum sample was found DA-free. To imitate real-world conditions, DA in the range of $100\ \mathrm{nM-}1.0\ \mathrm{mM}$ was spiked into the serum samples. Then, POM- $\boldsymbol{\upgamma}\mathrm{CD}$ MOF/LSGE was used to test the DA-spiked samples. This study was conducted to determine the increased levels of DA in the human body, which are linked to conditions that can severely affect the person’s health like Parkinson’s disease, schizophrenia, the growth of heart muscles, and blood pressure (Huang et al., 2020; Senel et al. 2020a, 2020b). As shown in Fig. 4A–C, our MOF sensor displayed distinct and linear CV and DPV response signals to each concentration of DA-spiked serum samples, ranging from $100~\mathrm{{nM}}$ to $1000\upmu\mathrm{M}$ . A linear regression plot is prepared from DPV curves, and the corresponding equation is, $\mathrm{~I~}(\upmu\mathrm{A})=58.83$ [DA] ${(\upmu\mathrm{{M}})}-71.08\$ . Thus, the POM- $\boldsymbol{\cdot}\gamma\mathrm{CD}$ MOF/LSGE exhibits significant practical feasibility.
## 3.6. Real-time monitoring of in-situ DA production in neuroblastoma cells
Next, the POM- $\boldsymbol{\cdot}\gamma\mathrm{CD}$ MOF sensor was tested for its ability to detect DA produced in situ by SH SY5Y cell lines of neuroblastoma cells. The $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ stimulation was applied to induce the cells to produce DA, which was detected by the POM- $\ensuremath{\gamma}\ensuremath{\mathrm{CD}}$ MOF/LSGE sensor (Fig. 5A) (Mir et al., 2015; Senel et al., 2020b). DPV experiments were performed with SH SY5Y live cells with and without KCl stimulations (Fig. 5B–D). The solutions containing only KCl demonstrated stable background current when tested with the sensor, except for some insignificant shift originating from the increased concentrations of $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ and $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ ions. Interestingly, a notable current signal enhancement was observed in the DPV of the solution containing cells incubated with KCl previously. Two different concentrations of KCl (10 and $15\ \mathrm{mM}$ were tested, and the signal increased as the incubation time increased. When cells and KCl had additional interaction time, they produced excessive DA. However, DA production halted at a certain time, suggesting that the cells may have sensed excessive DA in the environment. The patterns of DA production were slightly different when different concentrations of stimulant were used. For example, the real-time data for $10\mathrm{mM}$ KCl and $15\mathrm{mM}$ KCl was slightly different, particularly as saturation occurred at different time points. Faster saturation occurred when a high concentration of KCl was used. Next, the concentrations of DA produced in situ were calculated and are given in Table 1. Based on this table, we can infer that it is possible to quantitatively detect in situ DA using the POM- $\boldsymbol{\gamma}\mathrm{CD}$ MOF sensor, the results agree with previous reports (Emran et al., 2018; Huang et al., 2020). Most importantly, patterns of the in-situ DA release can be tracked, such patterns provide enormous information about the physiological and pathophysiological properties of the neural cells. The success of the sensor to detect cellular in-situ DA inferring that, this method may also be useful in monitoring DA release in DA-releasing drugs, which would open doors for applications in drug screening and pharmacokinetics.
However, we were unable to observe a color change with live cell experiments as the concentrations of DA production occurred at submicromolar levels. These concentrations must be approximately $50~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ to observe the visual signals. Further re-design of molecules may be needed to push the visual detection limit down to the levels of in situ DA. Nevertheless, the electrochemical signal readouts were highly sensitive to the DA produced by SH-SY5Y cells, which allowed us to perform realtime quantitative DA monitoring.
## 3.7. K-DopaSens: an integrated smartphone biosensor
We used the color-eliciting electrocatalytic interaction of DA with POM- $\boldsymbol{\cdot}\gamma\mathrm{CD}$ MOF/LSGE to develop a smartphone-operable biosensing platform. Smartphone-based biosensors are expected to play important role in diagnostic applications due to their ubiquity, user-friendliness, and familiarity among people (Quesada-Gonza´lez and Merkoçi, 2017). Smartphones today resemble mini-computers and feature operating systems, internal memory, and superior camera lenses (Ques­ ada-Gonza´lez and Merkoçi, 2017). The smartphone was used as a signal read-out in this study to quantify DA by reading the color of the solution. We developed a smartphone sensing platform named $K$ -DopaSens, which is a simple app easily operable via smartphone. The training dataset preparation process and the framework of the application are outlined in Fig. S11. This web-based app takes a picture of the electro-catalyzed sample as an input and predicts the corresponding concentration as the output; the output is determined a linear regression model trained using a set of pictures of samples with known concentrations to indi­ viduate the appropriate color.
The color of the sample is extracted by filtering the input image using the HSV (hue, saturation, and value) color space with certain thresholds. A series of experiments were performed with a different range of con­ centrations, and the images of the resulting DA analyses were used to create the $K$ -DopaSens web platform. The pixel extractions from a series of concentrations are displayed in Fig. S12. For each concentration, four sets of experiments were performed to maintain the accuracy of the readout. The use of $K$ -DopaSens to obtain visual and digital signals was presented in a video format (supporting video file).
The analysis and the use of K-DopaSens are user-friendly, and even non-technicians can operate this platform. The entire process involves the following quick and simple steps: (1) electrochemical treatment of DA with our sensors, (2) capturing the image with a smartphone camera, and (3) uploading the image on $K$ -DopaSens web platform. The $K$ -Dop­ aSens processes the image in a few seconds and translates quantitative information about DA. Because of its user-friendly, rapid, and sensitive detections, the POM- $\boldsymbol{\cdot}\gamma\mathrm{CD}$ MOF-integrated $K$ -DopaSens platform holds significant potential for point-of-care (PoC) analysis. DA concentrations of $50\upmu\mathrm{M}-1.0\up m\mathrm{M}$ can be quantified using this platform. Less than $50\upmu\mathrm{M}$ did not yield consistent results because of poor distinction with colors, but we are continuously working on a solution to this problem. Never­ theless, this platform introduces an interesting approach for DA de­ tections at PoC by providing both visual and quantitative detections in less than $5\mathrm{{min}}$ .
## 4. Conclusions
In conclusion, the structure of POM- $\boldsymbol{\gamma}\mathrm{CD}$ MOF combining macrocy­ clic MBBs $\mathbf{\dot{\boldsymbol{y}}}$ -cyclodextrin) and isolated polyoxomolybdates, was suc­ cessfully assembled through the bridging effect of counter-cations. The POM- $\boldsymbol{\cdot}\gamma\mathrm{CD}$ MOF is an excellent electrocatalyst, providing not only elec­ trochemical signals but also colorimetric signals. POM- $\boldsymbol{\upgamma}\mathrm{CD}$ MOF in as­ sociation with electrochemical perturbation elicits color and this property can be used to develop a highly selective dopamine sensor. POM- $\boldsymbol{\cdot}\gamma\mathrm{CD}$ MOF integrated smartphone sensor holds significant potential for user-friendly, PoC diagnostic applications. The material able for realtime in situ dopamine profiling released from neuroblastoma cells. The current version of the sensor unable to detect the color changes in cells because the concentrations of cellular dopamine are so low; however, the future work is directed towards tuning the supramolecular structure for visual detection of cellular level dopamine.
